ormous field of burgundy violet and black points traveling toward the opposite shoulder. Dot density creates waves depth and movement without drawing conventional strands. From far away the composition reads as a portrait surrounded by flowing darkness. Up close the entire environment breaks into thousands of points','Create the ultimate contemporary Dotwork tattoo across the mans shoulder chest and arm. A human figure emerges from dense black stippling on the chest. As the artwork reaches the shoulder the figure transitions into a raven whose wings wrap naturally around the deltoid. The bird then progressively disintegrates into thousands of points flowing down the upper arm. Within that field restrained cobalt dots gradually appear and organize themselves into an ocean-like atmospheric system before becoming sparse and disappearing near the forearm. There should be no obvious rectangular composition and no decorative field of random dots. Density direction anatomy and negative skin must control the entire piece. From several feet away it should read as one extraordinary atmospheric tattoo. Up close it should reveal that nearly all of its light shadow texture and transitions were constructed from individual points')
$b['A single sphere exists entirely through dot density. There is no outline. One side approaches near-black through tightly packed points while the opposite edge becomes so sparse that the sphere simply disappears into natural skin'] = 'The tattoo: A single sphere exists entirely through dot density. There is no outline. One side approaches near-black through tightly packed points while the opposite edge becomes so sparse that the sphere simply disappears into natural skin. Pure, quiet, dimensional. Create exceptional contemporary Dotwork tattoo artwork using individual dots as a fundamental image-making medium. Dotwork is a technique, not a limitation on subject matter. The artwork may depict people, portraits, human figures, animals, birds, insects, botanicals, ocean, landscapes, celestial imagery, geometry, ornament, architecture, abstraction, surreal imagery or entirely atmospheric compositions. Do not automatically generate mandalas, sacred geometry, moths or ornamental patterns simply because the requested style is Dotwork. BUILD THE IMAGE WITH DOTS. DO NOT CREATE AN ORDINARY TATTOO AND THEN SPRINKLE DOTS AROUND IT. Use controlled changes in dot spacing and density to construct light, shadow, volume, texture, depth and atmosphere. Dense points create darkness. Increasingly open points create lighter values. Natural skin should function as the brightest light. Allow forms to emerge from dot fields, disappear into them and sometimes exist without conventional outlines. At normal viewing distance the dots should optically combine into coherent forms and tonal transitions. At close range the individual point structure should remain visually meaningful. Design specifically for the stated body placement. The body determines the composition. Give intricate stippling enough physical scale and spacing to remain readable. Use forearms and calves for elongated subjects and gradients. Use shoulders for radial movement, dissolution and wrapping compositions. Use thighs for detailed portraits, animals and larger pointillist work. Use sternums for vertical or symmetrical structures. Use backs for large atmospheric fields and expansive compositions. Use ribs for elongated movement and figure work. Avoid forcing extremely dense microscopic stippling into placements too small to support it. BLACK AND NATURAL SKIN ARE FUNDAMENTAL TO DOTWORK, BUT COLOR IS FULLY ALLOWED. Use color when it makes the artwork better. Explore burgundy portraiture, coral botanicals, cobalt ocean, turquoise feathers, emerald animals, violet atmosphere, orange sunsets, red figures and sophisticated combinations of colored stippling. Color should be constructed through points and density rather than behaving like conventional smooth color fill underneath black stippling. Most color pieces should use controlled palettes. Some designs may use one color. Some may combine black with one or two deliberate colors. A smaller number may become genuine multicolor pointillist compositions. Do not turn every piece into rainbow stippling. Avoid repetitive mandalas, generic sacred geometry, endless moons, repetitive moths, random stippling surrounding ordinary linework, muddy grey fields, microscopic dots with insufficient spacing, decorative speckles, generic Pinterest imagery and normal smooth-shaded tattoos falsely labeled Dotwork. Use dot direction as well as density. Points may drift, spiral, dissolve, expand, compress, flow with wind, follow water, describe muscle, reveal curvature or carry movement around the body. Some designs can combine Dotwork with fine line, geometry, ornament or restrained black structure when the concept requires it, but the dot-built tonal language must remain central. Most designs should remain practical small and medium tattoos with adequate room for the technique. Use fewer large pieces and only rare major body compositions. THE DOT IS THE MEDIUM. THE SUBJECT CAN BE ANYTHING. THE BODY DETERMINES THE COMPOSITION. DOT DENSITY CREATES THE LIGHT. In the right panel it is on a woman, inner forearm, where the calm surface lets the tonal illusion remain clean'
